\section*{A new-Lefschetz defect reduction for rational Hodge pairings}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 001; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that reduction of rational Hodge classes on abelian varieties preserves rational intersection numbers with divisor products, in crystalline as well as prime-to-characteristic cohomology, including characteristic two; In manuscript 2 (family 032; 2026-09-30), the asserted result is that rational Hodge classes on CM abelian varieties in all dimensions are algebraic, with the stated Tate and Hodge-standard consequences; In manuscript 3 (family 032; 2026-09-30), the asserted result is that the rational Hodge assertion holds on every self-power of the stated hyperbolic Weil sixfolds of signature $(3,3)$ and on every self-power of imaginary-quadratic-action varieties of dimension at most five.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 001; 2026-09-23), the construction moves Hodge tensors through integral models of Hodge-type Shimura varieties while retaining a chosen divisor. An endomorphism-compatible filtration and balanced Weil classes reduce the comparison to CM varieties, where algebraic cycles supply the common rational value.

For manuscript 2 (family 032; 2026-09-30), balanced CM tensors are reduced to four-factor switch relations. Theta one-forms on compact ball quotients supply algebraic representatives for those relations, and polarization contractions assemble the general tensor before rational coefficient descent.

For manuscript 3 (family 032; 2026-09-30), a tensor criterion reduces algebraic supply to determinant classes at the given Hodge group. A geometric seed supplies the Weil plane, twisted-sheaf deformation transports it locally, and Hilbert spaces with a Baire argument extend algebraicity to all marked periods.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting results are manuscript 1 (family 001; 2026-09-23), manuscript 2 (family 032; 2026-09-30). The manuscript also gives additional strength beyond the requested conclusion; that excess is not required for this problem's solved-claim label. Other linked papers contribute only their stated special cases; those weaker results alone would not resolve the complete question.

